**Analysis.** For the value 7 the diamond has 7 lines with 1, 3, 5, 7, 5, 3, 1 stars: the pyramid of Task 2 (lines 1 to 4, from 1 to 7 stars) followed by the same lines in reverse order below the widest line (5, 3, 1 stars). In the example each star is followed by a blank, so a cell is two characters wide; the blanks in front of a line are counted in cells.

**Algorithm (pseudocode)**

```text
Initialise(MaxNoOfStars, NoOfSpaces, NoOfStars)       // MaxNoOfStars = the odd number entered
WHILE NoOfStars <= MaxNoOfStars DO                      // upper half, widest line included
    OutputLeadingSpaces(NoOfSpaces)
    OutputLineOfStars(NoOfStars)
    Adjust(NoOfSpaces, NoOfStars)                      // spaces - 1, stars + 2
END WHILE
AdjustBack twice                                        // leave the overshoot, skip the widest line
WHILE NoOfStars >= 1 DO                                 // lower half
    OutputLeadingSpaces(NoOfSpaces)
    OutputLineOfStars(NoOfStars)
    AdjustBack(NoOfSpaces, NoOfStars)                  // spaces + 1, stars - 2
END WHILE
```

**Modified program (ANSI C)** - `Initialise` and `Adjust` are unchanged; `OutputLeadingSpaces` and `OutputLineOfStars` now print two characters per cell, and `AdjustBack` is new:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int MaxNoOfStars;      /* the odd number entered = stars on the widest line */
int NoOfSpaces;
int NoOfStars;

void Initialise(int *MaxNoOfStars, int *NoOfSpaces, int *NoOfStars)
{
    int base = 0;

    printf("Enter the size of the base (an odd positive integer): ");
    scanf("%d", &base);
    while (base < 1 || base % 2 == 0)       /* reject even or non-positive values */
    {
        printf("The base must be an odd positive integer. Try again: ");
        scanf("%d", &base);
    }
    *MaxNoOfStars = base;                   /* stars on the last line (the base) */
    *NoOfSpaces = (base - 1) / 2;           /* the first line is centred over the base */
    *NoOfStars = 1;                         /* the first line has a single star */
}

void OutputLeadingSpaces(int nSpaces)  /* one cell = 2 characters ("* ") */
{
    int i;

    for (i = 1; i <= nSpaces; i++)
        printf("  ");
}

void OutputLineOfStars(int nStars)
{
    int i;

    for (i = 1; i <= nStars; i++)
        printf("* ");
    printf("\n");
}

void Adjust(int *NoOfSpaces, int *NoOfStars)
{
    *NoOfSpaces = *NoOfSpaces - 1;          /* one blank less ... */
    *NoOfStars = *NoOfStars + 2;            /* ... and two more stars */
}

void AdjustBack(int *NoOfSpaces, int *NoOfStars)   /* the opposite of Adjust */
{
    *NoOfSpaces = *NoOfSpaces + 1;
    *NoOfStars = *NoOfStars - 2;
}

int main(void)
{
    Initialise(&MaxNoOfStars, &NoOfSpaces, &NoOfStars);   /* reads the odd number */

    /* upper half, down to and including the widest line */
    while (NoOfStars <= MaxNoOfStars)
    {
        OutputLeadingSpaces(NoOfSpaces);
        OutputLineOfStars(NoOfStars);
        Adjust(&NoOfSpaces, &NoOfStars);
    }

    /* here NoOfSpaces = -1 and NoOfStars = MaxNoOfStars + 2 (one step too far) */
    AdjustBack(&NoOfSpaces, &NoOfStars);    /* back on the widest line */
    AdjustBack(&NoOfSpaces, &NoOfStars);    /* the first line below it */

    /* lower half */
    while (NoOfStars >= 1)
    {
        OutputLeadingSpaces(NoOfSpaces);
        OutputLineOfStars(NoOfStars);
        AdjustBack(&NoOfSpaces, &NoOfStars);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

**Why it works.** The first loop is the pyramid loop of Task 2 and stops with `NoOfSpaces = -1`, `NoOfStars = MaxNoOfStars + 2`. One `AdjustBack` returns to the widest line (0 blanks, MaxNoOfStars stars), a second one gives the first line below it (1 blank cell, MaxNoOfStars - 2 stars). The second loop prints the lines down to 1 star: after the line with 1 star (3 blank cells), `AdjustBack` gives 4 blank cells and `NoOfStars = -1`, so the test `NoOfStars >= 1` stops it.

**Trace for the value 7** (blank cells, stars): upper half (3,1) (2,3) (1,5) (0,7), lower half (1,5) (2,3) (3,1). Output of the program for 7 (obtained by running a faithful Python translation of the code; the text below shows the two-character cells):

```text
